WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:09.170
Okay, thank you very much. NHS Performance
Report, Barbara.

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:13.980
Barbara Hakin : Thank you, Chairman. Despite
being called the NHS Performance Report, this

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:18.350
report is predominantly about access standards
and we all know that there are many other

00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:23.949
features of performance which are really important
to patients, such as their experience and

00:00:23.949 --> 00:00:28.820
the clinical outcomes they receive. However,
nonetheless, access to services is of high

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:32.240
profile and the report focuses on that.

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:36.680
I am not going to read the report or make
reference to anything specific in it; I am

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:39.340
going to assume that the reports have been
read. However, I would just like to make two

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:43.650
points of context, if I may, Chairman, before
the discussion.

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:49.510
The first really is some numbers to show just
what a marvellous job our NHS have done, under

00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:53.730
a lot of pressure. We know the service is
under enormous pressure and yet they have

00:00:53.730 --> 00:01:00.120
still managed to deliver very, very good standards
to patients and the example is always the

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:06.970
A&amp;E standard, whereby we are still ensuring
that nine out of ten patients are treated

00:01:06.970 --> 00:01:11.380
- not seen but treated - within four hours
of transmission, they are either discharged

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:14.740
or admitted; in fact the median time for that
is 50 minutes.

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:19.670
However, the context I want to put behind
that is just how many more patients our NHS

00:01:19.670 --> 00:01:25.289
is treating now than it was some years ago.
So, just a few examples, we are now seeing

00:01:25.289 --> 00:01:32.289
2 million more patients attending our A&amp;E
services than five years ago, roughly 250,000

00:01:32.299 --> 00:01:37.670
more emergency admissions than just two years
ago, and in terms of our routine operations

00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:44.670
and procedures, 1 million more operations
and procedures than five years ago and 500,000

00:01:45.819 --> 00:01:52.819
more than a couple of years ago. So our NHS
is actually delivering outpatient care, planned

00:01:53.030 --> 00:02:00.030
care, routine care for something like 320,000
patients every month. 15% more ambulances

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:08.209
arrive at emergency situations than did five
years ago and 8% more ambulances arrive at

00:02:08.209 --> 00:02:15.080
emergency situations than did two years ago
and a staggering increase of 400% in the number

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:22.080
of 111 calls that we are now taking, compared
to 2012, so 12.5 million patients now actually

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:28.670
accessing this year NHS 111. So I think really
we want to just use this report to pay tribute

00:02:28.670 --> 00:02:35.510
to the NHS staff and say that, actually, those
are amazing figures in times of economic constraint

00:02:35.510 --> 00:02:40.269
that we have managed to see and treat many
more patients who need our care, so that was

00:02:40.269 --> 00:02:41.819
my first point of context.

00:02:41.819 --> 00:02:46.150
The second point of context I would just like
to make is about the tables because, while

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:52.409
we feel it is really important for transparency
to demonstrate where, in particular parts

00:02:52.409 --> 00:02:57.920
of the country, patients get their treatment
more quickly and where they do not, two of

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:04.060
the tables, I think, do deserve some explanation.
The first is the one on diagnostics and just

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:10.310
to let everyone be aware that this can be
very volatile; you know, a trust or a health

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:14.620
economy which normally provides good services
can have problems, suddenly, with a big piece

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:20.370
of equipment or a small number of staff which
can suddenly change these numbers quite quickly,

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:23.689
so I think they have to be treated at any
point in time.

00:03:23.689 --> 00:03:30.689
Finally, some context for the 62 day urgent
cancer referral centres: many of the organisations

00:03:32.109 --> 00:03:38.530
that we see with the low numbers are complex,
tertiary centres. They are therefore seeing

00:03:38.530 --> 00:03:43.299
the patients with the greatest need, often
who have been referred from other local hospitals

00:03:43.299 --> 00:03:47.620
and these organisations have only received
the patients towards the end of that period.

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:50.170
I just think it is really important that if
we are putting these in the public domain,

00:03:50.170 --> 00:03:50.269
we put that context behind them.

00:03:50.269 --> 00:03:55.209
Malcolm Grant: Yes, thank you; those are valuable
caveats for us. Comments? Ciaran?

00:03:55.209 --> 00:04:02.209
Ciaran Devane: I agree with you; performance
wise, it is fantastic. I think the doing bit

00:04:02.519 --> 00:04:08.620
of the NHS has done an amazing job. Does the
thinking bit of the NHS really understand

00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:13.260
why we are seeing additional people, what
are their characteristics, what decisions

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:19.109
are they making, what their behaviours are?
Do we have the right level of analysis behind

00:04:19.109 --> 00:04:22.400
that and if not, can we have it please?

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:27.830
Barbara Hakin: I think we have a broad understanding
of the main categories: people are getting

00:04:27.830 --> 00:04:33.750
older, they live with many more conditions,
the health service is much better able to

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:37.830
do things for patients, there are many more
treatments that are available. Additionally,

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:43.220
we have not seen an investment in primary
and community services in the way we have

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:48.919
in acute trust services, hospital services,
therefore we see that drift. Furthermore,

00:04:48.919 --> 00:04:52.819
obviously in the last half hour or so we have
talked about the cuts to social care. There

00:04:52.819 --> 00:04:58.050
is therefore a range of factors that we understand
are all contributing to this increase in demand

00:04:58.050 --> 00:05:03.220
but we absolutely feel that we have not got
enough analysis on that and we have initiated

00:05:03.220 --> 00:05:07.780
a piece of work, nationally, to try and understand
which particular one of those factors is a

00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:14.030
key driver. However, that is not to underestimate
the need for that to be done in detail locally

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:18.520
as well because what we do see is an enormous
variation in some of these numbers, when you

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:23.780
drill into them locally, into the changes
in demand for both urgents and electives.

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:30.090
Ciaran Devane: I guess if, in other sectors,
you are saying this is the kind of measurement

00:05:30.090 --> 00:05:34.789
you are doing all the time because your population
is different to the place next door, they

00:05:34.789 --> 00:05:39.009
are different this year to six months ago
and they will be different again in six months'

00:05:39.009 --> 00:05:45.110
time. So, are we in a position to get that
kind of real time insight and monitoring;

00:05:45.110 --> 00:05:50.280
Tim would probably say the phrase �market
research', as opposed to �academic research'?

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:55.400
So rather than doing one big analysis for
this year and then coming back to it in five

00:05:55.400 --> 00:06:01.020
years' time, can we build a system which kind
of does it routinely, it is just normal business

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:01.599
intelligence?

00:06:01.599 --> 00:06:07.000
Barbara Hakin: I think that there would be
quite a lot of work to do to build a system

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:11.780
that does this economy by economy. I think
we are on the basis of building it at national

00:06:11.780 --> 00:06:17.139
level but there would be a lot to do for people
to build that routine monitoring but the tool

00:06:17.139 --> 00:06:22.949
we will do nationally will support them to
do that. On the point you make about what

00:06:22.949 --> 00:06:29.949
is happening: something like the campaigns
about cancer, which caused a very significant

00:06:31.379 --> 00:06:36.720
rise in people coming forward to be diagnosed,
which is why we have done many more diagnostic

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:42.400
tests. We need to be much better than we have
been in the past at being ahead of that. That

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:49.400
sort of campaign is fantastic, we want demand
for diagnosis to grow, but we need to make

00:06:50.319 --> 00:06:55.819
sure that we are predicting those trends enough
to put the capacity in place, rather than

00:06:55.819 --> 00:06:59.710
actually having to put the capacity in place
once it has happened.

00:06:59.710 --> 00:07:05.860
Malcolm Grant: Good. Barbara, thank you very
much for that and I echo your words about

00:07:05.860 --> 00:07:12.580
how remarkable the performance has been in
terms of numbers coming through the system.

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:14.810
Right, thank you very much.

00:07:14.810 --> 00:07:19.379
May we move on then, please, to the next report?
Barbara, it is back to you on Emergency Preparedness,

00:07:19.379 --> 00:07:20.669
Resilience and Response.

00:07:20.669 --> 00:07:25.190
Barbara Hakin: Thank you. Again, I know that
the board members will have read the paper

00:07:25.190 --> 00:07:30.060
but if I can emphasise, I think this is a
really important paper. It is one of NHS England's

00:07:30.060 --> 00:07:36.240
most critical functions to make sure that
the NHS is prepared for any emergency; fortunately,

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:41.470
it is one where most of what we do is preparation
and it is rarely needed.

00:07:41.470 --> 00:07:45.849
I think the paper does two things, I hope.
I think the most important thing is it asks

00:07:45.849 --> 00:07:51.659
whether it provides sufficient assurance to
you, as a board, that the function that we

00:07:51.659 --> 00:07:58.659
perform, on behalf of the NHS is adequate;
I think there are a number of pieces of evidence

00:07:59.389 --> 00:08:03.759
here which give good assurance. I think that
the auditors' internal audit report on this

00:08:03.759 --> 00:08:10.060
is very reassuring but I think on the wider
range of assurance that we do across government

00:08:10.060 --> 00:08:16.060
with this, the evidence is there and I would
like to commend the team that does this for

00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:16.940
that.

00:08:16.949 --> 00:08:22.930
The report also just reports on the activity
this year. It has been a very busy year for

00:08:22.930 --> 00:08:29.930
actually having incidents; we should not underestimate
the industrial action and things that have

00:08:30.039 --> 00:08:36.060
happened with Ebola have made this team very,
very busy indeed. So I think, whilst we commend,

00:08:36.060 --> 00:08:43.060
absolutely, all the NHS staff who have gone
to work in West Africa to support on Ebola,

00:08:43.110 --> 00:08:49.300
we also need to particularly commend the people
who have worked on the preparations for those

00:08:49.300 --> 00:08:54.440
patients with potential Ebola, or Ebola, who
have come back to the UK. We have a very small

00:08:54.440 --> 00:08:58.430
team in NHS England who have done this, who
have done a fantastic job and a small number

00:08:58.430 --> 00:09:03.209
of our hospitals have been ready to receive
patients and obviously, in the case of the

00:09:03.209 --> 00:09:07.779
Royal Free, have done so. So I think this
report is testament to how well that has been

00:09:07.779 --> 00:09:13.220
done and hopefully gives you adequate assurance
that, across the broader range of emergency

00:09:13.220 --> 00:09:16.649
planning, we can meet our statutory duties
and the report that we will need to make to

00:09:16.649 --> 00:09:18.350
the Department of Health.

00:09:18.350 --> 00:09:23.250
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much Barbara.
And I think it would be really useful if members

00:09:23.250 --> 00:09:28.399
of the board who have not visited the team
based in this building were to find an opportunity

00:09:28.399 --> 00:09:34.000
to do so. I think it is really quite eye opening:
the array of responsibilities that they have,

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:40.410
the need to be able to provide rapid response
and to take control of emergency situations

00:09:40.410 --> 00:09:43.600
anywhere in the country at any time.

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:49.380
I do not detect comments so, Barbara, may
we thank you very much for that report.

00:09:49.380 --> 00:09:54.589
We move on, Karen, to our own business plan
and how well we have done.

00:09:54.589 --> 00:09:57.450
Karen Wheeler: So, very briefly, this report
just provides an overview of how we have done

00:09:57.450 --> 00:10:04.450
against the business plan of this current
year and it provides an update and an overview

00:10:09.940 --> 00:10:15.370
across all the 31 business areas. It is just
worth reminding people those 31 business areas,

00:10:15.370 --> 00:10:21.389
more or less, cover everything that NHS England
does, so it includes change as well as operational

00:10:21.389 --> 00:10:28.389
performance. So, some of the areas which have
been marked as amber red or red are there

00:10:29.850 --> 00:10:33.570
largely because of some of the operational
pressures that we have received, rather than

00:10:33.570 --> 00:10:38.630
failure to deliver. There has been a lot of
very successful delivery underpinning all

00:10:38.630 --> 00:10:39.660
of the 31 business areas.

00:10:39.660 --> 00:10:46.660
I was not going to say anything more about
that, or the major programmes, or the corporate

00:10:48.600 --> 00:10:55.600
risks; I just wanted to assure you that we
are providing good assurance through all of

00:10:57.139 --> 00:11:04.019
those programmes and through our monthly risk
reporting which, obviously, addresses risks

00:11:04.019 --> 00:11:07.970
as they affect our corporate delivery. That
was all I wanted to say.

00:11:07.970 --> 00:11:12.480
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much and those
final comments remind us that this is not,

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:16.360
as it were, a sort of end of year report;
it is something that has been tracked consistently

00:11:16.360 --> 00:11:21.779
through the year through Ed's committee and
also through reports to the main board.

00:11:21.779 --> 00:11:25.930
I love seeing charts where there are patches
of green because that is quite an innovation

00:11:25.930 --> 00:11:28.010
and very welcome.

00:11:28.010 --> 00:11:31.560
Victor Adebowale: Spring is in the air.

00:11:31.560 --> 00:11:34.180
Malcolm Grant: What we then do, of course,
is wipe out those risks and concentrate on

00:11:34.180 --> 00:11:38.320
the red ones, which is the proper process
of evolution.

00:11:38.320 --> 00:11:43.410
Right, on the basis that there are no further
comments on that, can we thank you, Karen,

00:11:43.410 --> 00:11:46.519
for that report that we receive and note.

00:11:46.519 --> 00:11:50.240
Paul, now we turn to the consolidated financial
report, over to you.

00:11:50.240 --> 00:11:54.779
Paul Baumann: Malcolm, I will keep this very
brief for two reasons, one of which is, that

00:11:54.779 --> 00:11:58.290
whilst I welcome the wisdom of the board on
all financial matters, given that we have

00:11:58.290 --> 00:12:03.029
three working days left in '14/'15, my time
to implement such wisdom is probably fairly

00:12:03.029 --> 00:12:05.149
limited.

00:12:05.149 --> 00:12:11.470
The other thing to say is that we talked about
this at quite some length at month eight.

00:12:11.470 --> 00:12:15.050
In fact the only thing that has changed since
month eight, in any substance, is something

00:12:15.050 --> 00:12:20.550
I foresaw at that time, which is the slower
pace of clearing the continuing healthcare

00:12:20.550 --> 00:12:26.079
legacy claims. This slower pace has led to
the release of some of the money that we provided

00:12:26.079 --> 00:12:32.750
to CCGs to settle those claims during '14/'15
to the tune of about £150 million and as

00:12:32.750 --> 00:12:37.290
you can see, that has worked its way through
into the financial numbers for this year.

00:12:37.290 --> 00:12:42.300
However, clearly the big question we are working
on at the moment is: does that lead to additional

00:12:42.300 --> 00:12:47.110
expenditure next year, which would give us
a further problem in a year in which we have

00:12:47.110 --> 00:12:51.380
significant challenges as it is, or is there
any indication that, actually, the rate of

00:12:51.380 --> 00:12:58.160
settlement is lower than we had anticipated
and therefore there is no such adverse impact?

00:12:58.160 --> 00:13:02.350
However, broadly speaking I can reassure the
board: we are landing the jumbo jet more or

00:13:02.350 --> 00:13:09.350
less on the postage stamp; the 0.2% variance
that you can see on the charts here is pretty

00:13:09.360 --> 00:13:14.740
close to a point landing, it is just about
on the right side of the point landing, which

00:13:14.740 --> 00:13:20.589
is always good. I would not want to underestimate,
however, the pressures, when you strip away

00:13:20.589 --> 00:13:24.190
things like the continuing healthcare that
I have just talked about and the fact that

00:13:24.190 --> 00:13:28.750
we have been able to bear down on our running
costs and our central programme costs, to

00:13:28.750 --> 00:13:32.920
some extent in preparation for a very lean
year on both of those fronts next year. When

00:13:32.920 --> 00:13:37.170
you strip all of that away, the underlying
financial dynamics in the commissioning system,

00:13:37.170 --> 00:13:42.760
just as it is in the providing system, have
been extremely tough and are extremely tough,

00:13:42.760 --> 00:13:48.649
which is why the planning process that we
are going through at the moment is, I think,

00:13:48.649 --> 00:13:53.930
the toughest that I have seen in quite a number
of years and will take us another few weeks

00:13:53.930 --> 00:13:57.259
to get through. However, that is probably
as much as I need to say today.

00:13:57.259 --> 00:14:00.120
Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Paul. Ed, do you
wish to add to that?

00:14:00.120 --> 00:14:06.800
Ed Smith: No, I think the way in which this
has been managed and the way in which NHS

00:14:06.800 --> 00:14:13.800
England has coped with the additional cost
pressures in year by managing the other things

00:14:16.480 --> 00:14:22.610
down has been tremendous and I think, looking
forward to next year, this is going to be

00:14:22.610 --> 00:14:28.790
a lot, lot tougher, so we may as well as be
ready for not quite such a way in which it

00:14:28.790 --> 00:14:31.810
can be worked out in the second half of the
year.

00:14:31.810 --> 00:14:36.959
Malcolm Grant: Good. Well, thank you very
much. I propose now to move on to the reports

00:14:36.959 --> 00:14:42.730
from committees. Of two of them, I do not
think we wish to open the investment committee

00:14:42.730 --> 00:14:48.180
because it is a starred item and I have not
heard anything to the contrary. Secondly,

00:14:48.180 --> 00:14:49.920
audit and risk, likewise.

00:14:49.920 --> 00:14:56.130
Fine, well that allows me then to invite David
Roberts to give us a brief oral briefing on

00:14:56.130 --> 00:15:02.430
the Commissioning Committee that held its
first substantive meeting yesterday. David?

00:15:02.430 --> 00:15:08.040
David Roberts: The Commissioning Committee
met for three hours yesterday and focused

00:15:08.040 --> 00:15:15.040
on its normal four standing items of strategy
assurance, finance and quality. Three key

00:15:16.519 --> 00:15:23.050
themes, I think, go across all of those. The
first is support, so how can we support the

00:15:23.050 --> 00:15:30.050
various elements of the system to get things
done and to improve? The second is learning,

00:15:30.820 --> 00:15:36.610
so what can we learn from all the various
initiatives? And the third is scalability,

00:15:36.610 --> 00:15:43.610
how do we scale these issues when we see things
that work? Those three themes, I think, will

00:15:44.620 --> 00:15:49.069
be perennial themes in the way the committee
thinks about and operates and fulfils its

00:15:49.069 --> 00:15:54.459
mandate. There was an awful lot that we did,
so I am more than happy for board members

00:15:54.459 --> 00:16:01.459
to take more detailed observations and thoughts
and questions and requests for more information

00:16:02.050 --> 00:16:04.160
outside of the board.

00:16:04.160 --> 00:16:09.060
Under strategy, we started with the new models
of care; Ian has broadly covered that earlier.

00:16:09.060 --> 00:16:13.899
However, I think the comment we would make
there is just to re-emphasise that we are

00:16:13.899 --> 00:16:20.579
focused on: what are the critical barriers
to getting these models of care up and running

00:16:20.579 --> 00:16:25.440
and how do we solve them? And secondly - and
critically - what do we learn and how do we

00:16:25.440 --> 00:16:28.509
turn them into scalable operations?

00:16:28.509 --> 00:16:32.899
We then had a conversation around primary
which fell into, broadly, three categories.

00:16:32.899 --> 00:16:39.069
The first was co commissioning, which, again,
Ian covered earlier; I think our comment there

00:16:39.069 --> 00:16:46.069
was really about evaluation and learning.
We have asked that we use the regional teams

00:16:48.500 --> 00:16:54.069
to understand the nature of conversations
and decisioning that is being taken in the

00:16:54.069 --> 00:16:58.720
co commissioning and joint commissioning areas,
again to see what we can learn and see how

00:16:58.720 --> 00:17:04.660
those arrangements are working. We then had
a conversation about where next, if you like,

00:17:04.660 --> 00:17:09.650
in terms of co commissioning and I think that
inevitably takes us into some of the other

00:17:09.650 --> 00:17:16.650
areas of NHS England's responsibilities and
I think there is a drive for place based commissioning

00:17:17.860 --> 00:17:24.740
offset against the capacity and just sheer
change agenda that a number of the CCGs, in

00:17:24.740 --> 00:17:29.090
particular, are operating under, so that is
going to be a balance, I think, going forward.

00:17:29.090 --> 00:17:37.830
The third thing that we looked at under primary
care was a decision around the investments

00:17:37.910 --> 00:17:43.910
under the Prime Minister's second phase of
the Challenge Fund and also the money that

00:17:43.910 --> 00:17:50.500
was made available to support greater infrastructure
change within primary care, and we approved

00:17:50.500 --> 00:17:57.450
both the approach and the principles of how
that was done. Again, sorry to repeat, but

00:17:57.450 --> 00:18:01.970
what we want to understand is, with the first
wave of that investment, which is quite material

00:18:01.970 --> 00:18:08.790
sums of money, what can we learn, how can
we see and shape and improve, going forward?

00:18:08.790 --> 00:18:13.600
I think, standing back from all of that, we
asked ourselves a question: so, with some

00:18:13.600 --> 00:18:18.400
quite significant interventions across many
different areas, whether it be new models

00:18:18.400 --> 00:18:23.140
of care in their differing dimensions, whether
it be the Prime Minister's Challenge Fund,

00:18:23.140 --> 00:18:26.760
whether it be co commissioning, when you stand
back and look at all of this, what does the

00:18:26.760 --> 00:18:32.660
map look like? How can we actually see not
just whether each one, in isolation is proving

00:18:32.660 --> 00:18:39.100
to be successful or otherwise, but also how
do they multiply, as in the multiplier effect

00:18:39.100 --> 00:18:46.100
in economic terms; can we see the benefits
scaling or not, as the case may be? We have

00:18:47.620 --> 00:18:52.550
asked and Barbara and Ian have taken a challenge
away to look at the map and also to start

00:18:52.550 --> 00:18:59.550
to see whether we can create, maybe, local
areas, regions, where we do put multiple elements

00:18:59.550 --> 00:19:05.520
of change in, to see how that impact scales
or otherwise?

00:19:05.520 --> 00:19:12.210
We then also briefly reviewed Manchester and
asked ourselves the question: what does this

00:19:12.210 --> 00:19:17.820
mean for clinical commissioning and what does
it mean for commissioning more broadly? I

00:19:17.820 --> 00:19:22.160
think the conversation there was, again: how
do we support, how do we learn? However, I

00:19:22.160 --> 00:19:29.160
think the risk of losing clinical leadership
at the local level was perceived to be a material

00:19:29.790 --> 00:19:34.820
issue that we would want to understand and
make sure that the big, positive, benefits

00:19:34.820 --> 00:19:39.280
from clinical leadership were not negated
in any way; we do not think they are but we

00:19:39.280 --> 00:19:42.420
think there is a risk there that we want to
get close to.

00:19:42.420 --> 00:19:46.480
So, with all of that and with the various
differing initiatives, the final thing we

00:19:46.480 --> 00:19:53.480
looked at under strategy was that we are actually
embarking over a thought process, over the

00:19:55.130 --> 00:20:02.130
next four to six months, about how we create
a modern, efficient, supportive commissioning

00:20:05.160 --> 00:20:10.950
system, which is unlikely to have one means
of commissioning but many means of commissioning.

00:20:10.950 --> 00:20:16.750
We heard today the power of patients taking
responsibility for commissioning via their

00:20:16.750 --> 00:20:22.510
own, personalised budgets. How do we think
about that, relative to other means that we

00:20:22.510 --> 00:20:26.030
operate now and the various things? What are
the capabilities, what are the tools, what

00:20:26.030 --> 00:20:32.090
is the education that is needed to help make
that work and what are the metrics? We are

00:20:32.090 --> 00:20:36.690
therefore going to have a series of interventions,
basically every month between now and the

00:20:36.690 --> 00:20:41.920
end of October, probably, where we learn from
other parts of the world, we learn from our

00:20:41.920 --> 00:20:45.540
experience and we pull that together. And
I think that will be a topic that we might

00:20:45.540 --> 00:20:50.210
want to bring back to the board at various
chunks, once we have gone through, maybe,

00:20:50.210 --> 00:20:52.320
three months and then six months.

00:20:52.320 --> 00:20:57.780
So that was, broadly, commissioning strategy.
Commissioning assurance was probably the centrepiece

00:20:57.780 --> 00:21:04.780
of our conversation and we recognise we are
in a very different place on the CCG operations,

00:21:05.450 --> 00:21:11.550
we recognise that we need a new model for
assurance - the board has as a statutory responsibility

00:21:11.550 --> 00:21:17.250
for assurance; we were clear that this is
not an inspection regime, this is an assurance

00:21:17.250 --> 00:21:22.850
regime and it is a learning regime. We were
also clear and Barbara was very clear, this

00:21:22.850 --> 00:21:29.260
is a continuous process, it is not sort of
three people turning up on a wet Wednesday

00:21:29.260 --> 00:21:36.260
to inspect. As part of that, therefore, we
were asked to approve a new assurance strategy

00:21:38.400 --> 00:21:45.400
and process, which we did. Implicit in that
is judgement on a range of factors and a very

00:21:47.230 --> 00:21:53.100
differentiated, risk based approach to what
then may be the consequences of where people

00:21:53.100 --> 00:21:54.860
come out.

00:21:54.860 --> 00:22:02.360
We requested some changes in that and the
approach, which are being done and we also

00:22:02.630 --> 00:22:08.420
recognised that, as part of this process,
there is a scorecard being developed for CCGs

00:22:08.420 --> 00:22:12.340
which we are seeking views on. I think it
is important to recognise where we are in

00:22:12.340 --> 00:22:18.630
this process: we are seeking views, so we
are at an early stage of that process. I think

00:22:18.630 --> 00:22:25.630
our thoughts on this assurance process were
really three things. The first is being very

00:22:26.370 --> 00:22:31.330
clear on the facts, what are the facts, be
they qualitative or quantitative views? The

00:22:31.330 --> 00:22:37.560
second is being very open minded about the
reasons as to why the situation has occurred

00:22:37.560 --> 00:22:41.480
because we have very different contexts in
which we are operating? Finally, being very

00:22:41.480 --> 00:22:48.480
clear and very risk based on the consequences;
those consequences start with how we and other

00:22:49.220 --> 00:22:55.310
CCGs, through peer review, buddy systems,
etc., can help support and challenge CCGs

00:22:55.310 --> 00:23:01.040
to improve and how we can make sure they have
the capabilities and capacity to do that.

00:23:01.040 --> 00:23:05.920
We are therefore trying to move, again to
support, learning and leverage being the three

00:23:05.920 --> 00:23:07.350
key words.

00:23:07.350 --> 00:23:14.350
Finally, very briefly, we had a review of
the efficiency process that the team are working

00:23:14.910 --> 00:23:19.180
their way through. I would describe that as
a sighting shot, the exam question being:

00:23:19.180 --> 00:23:24.200
how can we use the commissioning process to
drive the efficiencies that we all need to

00:23:24.200 --> 00:23:31.200
have a sustainable and high quality care for
our patients? Then we had a, I think, very

00:23:33.140 --> 00:23:40.140
important conversation about Morecambe Bay,
led by Bruce and Jane and about understanding

00:23:42.860 --> 00:23:47.190
what the actions are we have committed to
and how we can make sure that the commissioning

00:23:47.190 --> 00:23:53.270
systems makes those happens but also, again,
what the learnings are which come out of that

00:23:53.270 --> 00:23:58.590
and from some other issues that have arisen
over the last years that are well understood.

00:23:58.590 --> 00:24:04.040
We are actually standing back and asking ourselves:
how can we use the commissioning system to

00:24:04.040 --> 00:24:09.530
try and drive the focus on quality in everything
that it does?

00:24:09.530 --> 00:24:10.430
Noel and Victor, anything I have missed?

00:24:10.430 --> 00:24:12.010
Victor Adebowale: Covered.

00:24:12.010 --> 00:24:16.930
David Roberts: Okay. So that is what we did,
I am happy to take any questions or indeed

00:24:16.930 --> 00:24:18.160
to follow up separately with people.

00:24:18.160 --> 00:24:21.980
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much, David,
I think that reinforces our confidence that

00:24:21.980 --> 00:24:25.550
we made the right decision in setting up this
committee; I think you are asking some of

00:24:25.550 --> 00:24:32.120
the very existential questions that we need
to review, so thank you for that. Can I invite

00:24:32.120 --> 00:24:39.120
any questions or comments on David's presentation?
No. Well, thank you very much and we will,

00:24:39.380 --> 00:24:42.360
I know, have a written paper in due course
and give us some opportunity to reflect further.

00:24:42.360 --> 00:24:48.420
Malcolm Grant: Right, well that brings us
towards the close of the meeting. Just before

00:24:48.420 --> 00:24:53.270
we do, I thought I should offer Simon an opportunity
to give us a summary of key messages that

00:24:53.270 --> 00:24:56.800
he can take away from the discussions this
morning.

00:24:56.800 --> 00:25:02.560
Simon Stevens: Yes. Well, I am not sure I
will adequately discharge that task, Malcolm,

00:25:02.560 --> 00:25:08.370
but I guess what strikes me, throughout this
conversation, is the series of dualities that

00:25:08.370 --> 00:25:13.230
we have to hold in our mind, both of them
at the same time. The first is that it is

00:25:13.230 --> 00:25:18.060
tough now, across many parts of the National
Health Service - we have to be realistic about

00:25:18.060 --> 00:25:24.740
the pressures and the constraints - but we
also have to be ambitious about the future

00:25:24.740 --> 00:25:31.740
and recognise that better is possible. We
have to make the case for a comprehensive,

00:25:31.750 --> 00:25:37.770
adequately resourced National Health Service
but we also have to recognise that we are

00:25:37.770 --> 00:25:43.020
not going to be able to spend our way to the
future on the existing ways of delivering

00:25:43.020 --> 00:25:47.880
services and so the time to get going with
the agenda we have set and which is broadly

00:25:47.880 --> 00:25:52.800
agreed is now. We know what that playbook
looks like, we know what the big debates are

00:25:52.800 --> 00:25:56.460
that we have to land: the health and social
care discussion that we have had, the service

00:25:56.460 --> 00:26:01.670
redesign discussion, whether it be genomics
or vanguards or the use of technology. We

00:26:01.670 --> 00:26:08.670
know that the efficiency agenda needs to be
landed and mapped very carefully, which we

00:26:10.200 --> 00:26:15.170
are doing with all of the other national bodies
in the National Health Service, as well as

00:26:15.170 --> 00:26:22.170
the partners in CCGs and local government
and providers and so it is obviously a huge

00:26:22.180 --> 00:26:27.600
agenda. For all the reasons that Barbara has
set out, the health service has actually done

00:26:27.600 --> 00:26:33.450
incredibly well over the course of not just
the last three months, six months, year but

00:26:33.450 --> 00:26:39.050
beyond, so we have an enormous amount to be
proud of. However, as a former Health Secretary

00:26:39.050 --> 00:26:46.050
used to frequently quote Gramsci when he said,
'It is combining the pessimism of the intellect

00:26:46.140 --> 00:26:48.220
with the optimism of the will.'

00:26:48.220 --> 00:26:53.350
Malcolm Grant: There could have been no more
elegant summing up than that. That is what

00:26:53.350 --> 00:26:57.570
we decided this morning, that is what we did.
So thank you very much for that.

00:26:57.570 --> 00:27:01.990
We have reached the stage of the meeting where
we close the public part of the meeting and

00:27:01.990 --> 00:27:06.290
we will go into private session in a moment.
However, can I thank everybody for their attendance

00:27:06.290 --> 00:27:09.670
this morning and for the contributions of
my colleagues around the table.

00:27:09.670 --> 00:27:11.240
Thank you very much, the meeting is closed.

